analysisWe need to read the comment and produce an assert statement.

Comment:

```
// @@@ If the starting index is greater than or equal to -1, then for every index greater than the result and less than or equal to the starting index, the bit at that index is set to true.
```

We are in method previousClearBit(int fromIndex). The method returns the index of the previous clear bit (false) before or at fromIndex. Actually previousClearBit returns the index of nearest bit set to false that occurs on or before fromIndex. If none, returns -1.

The comment says: If the starting index is greater than or equal to -1, then for every index greater than the result and less than or equal to the starting index, the bit at that index is set to true.

That is a postcondition: For all i such that result < i <= fromIndex, get(i) == true.

Premise: fromIndex >= -1. That's always true for any non-negative? Actually fromIndex can be -1 as per spec. So we assert: (fromIndex >= -1) => (\forall int i; result < i && i <= fromIndex; this.get(i))

But we need to express "bit at that index is set to true". So this.get(i) returns boolean true. So condition: this.get(i) == true, or just this.get(i). Use boolean expression.

Thus assert: assert fromIndex >= -1 => (\forall int i; \result < i && i <= fromIndex; this.get(i)); However need to reference result: \result.

In JML style, \result is used. So final assert:

assert (fromIndex >= -1) => \forall int i; \result < i && i <= fromIndex; this.get(i);

Make sure parentheses: assert (fromIndex >= -1) => (\forall int i; \result < i && i <= fromIndex; this.get(i));

That's it.

Check if need to use \old? No, we refer to final state after method. The get method reads current bit values after operation. The method does not modify bits, only reads. So fine.

Thus output code block.

